  "content": {
    "id": "v2-p10-practice-slot-7-upper-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "A bag contains red, blue and white counters.\n\n- The probability of picking red is $\\frac14$.\n- The probability of picking blue is $\\frac13$.\n- There are 10 white counters.\n\nHow many red counters are there?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "6 red counters.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{white})&=1-\\frac14-\\frac13\\\\P(\\text{white})&=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{align*}$$\nThe 10 white counters are $\\frac{5}{12}$ of the bag, so $\\frac{1}{12}$ of the bag is 2 counters and the bag holds $2\\times12=24$ counters.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Red counters}&=24\\times\\frac14\\\\\\text{Red counters}&=6\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
